v0.9.0: zero-cost fast path, language-agnostic flat glossary, domain packs

FOCUS: be a fast, lightweight decision engine — not a language framework.
No new dependency (declared deps unchanged: fastembed, numpy, scikit-learn).

1) Zero-cost fast path — the miss penalty is gone.
   v0.8.8 vectorized the query inside sparse_fastpath() and then AGAIN inside
   evaluate() whenever the gate declined, so every miss paid ~1-2 ms on top of
   the dense pass for nothing. decide() now builds one SparseQuery and hands the
   same object to the gate and to evaluate(encoded, sparse=...). Verified by
   instrumentation, not assertion: 1 build per decide() on hit AND on miss
   (tests/test_v090.py::TestFastpathVectorReuse, benchmark section 2).
   Measured: hit 0.499 ms median vs dense fallback 19.2 ms (~39x); miss 19.2 ms
   vs baseline-without-fastpath 37.6 ms — a miss is NOT slower.

   Backward compatibility: passing `sparse=` blind would raise TypeError on every
   custom BaseHead subclass written against the old signature. BaseHead probes
   _accepts_keyword once at construction; the engine passes it only when accepted.

2) Language-specific branches removed entirely.
   _guess_lang, _detect_lang, _GERMAN_SIGNAL_WORDS, _cross_lingual_mixup and the
   cross_lingual_only switch are gone. The glossary is a flat, language-agnostic
   lookup; its one language-dependent decision is made on EVIDENCE, not a guess:
   synonyms the anchors already use are not bridged. The skip set is _core_vocab
   (the anchors' OWN tokens) — NOT the full _vocab, which already holds the
   anchor-side glossary terms and would skip the very terms a foreign query
   needs (this subtlety broke the bridge when first implemented; caught by tests).
   classifier="auto" is now a deprecated alias for "centroid"; translate_fn gets
   its mirror language explicitly via translate_target=.

3) Glossary modularised + validated.
   klix.glossary keeps only the mechanism; klix.glossaries holds the packs:
   empty() (default), manufacturing() (the 16 production terms), workflow()
   (generic routing), default() (broad bundled vocabulary), merge_all().
   Choice(glossary=...) and DecisionEngine(glossary=...) accept dict | path |
   Glossary | None. Expansion is bounded (per_concept_topk, round-robin over the
   concept's languages, plus max_added) — the round-robin fixed a real failure
   where "urgent" yielded three English terms and one German one.
   Glossary.validate(anchors=None) reports duplicates, genuine circular
   mappings, homographs and anchor collisions. New matched_concepts metadata.

   Load path: the index build costs ~2.5x the JSON parse, so load_glossary()
   defers it to the first lookup (is_indexed exposes the state).

4) Broad bundled DE<->EN vocabulary (data/default_glossary.json).
   Source: Wikidata (CC0 1.0) — see DATA_SOURCES.md. NLTK's omw-1.4 was rejected
   because it ships NO German at all, and the OMW sets that exist are mostly
   CC BY-SA / CeCILL-C (share-alike vs. this package's MIT). Two routes:
   POS-tagged lexemes (noun/verb/adjective, so no name noise) + curated item
   classes. Deterministic (ORDER BY, on-disk response cache); deep class subtrees
   that exceed the WDQS timeout are skipped and reported, not fatal.

   10134 concepts / 23663 terms / 0.62 MB, validate() -> 0 findings.
   Size chosen from the measured curve (evals/glossary_footprint.py): 10k concepts
   is the largest point satisfying load, RAM and term-count budgets together;
   13.8k overshot the load budget.

5) Bugs found by the suite while building this (all fixed + pinned):
   - Score.evaluate/Flag.evaluate did not accept the new kwarg, breaking every
     mixed Choice+Score/Flag schema.
   - NameError 'row' in the reject pole (twice) and in the hard-negative penalty
     after the vectorization refactor.
   - Glossary.validate() produced 287 false positives (a term colliding only with
     another concept's synthetic KEY is not an ambiguous mapping).
   - The builder tracked term owners PER LANGUAGE, allowing the same word to be
     German in one concept and English in another — 287 genuine ambiguities.
     Now one owner per term across both languages.
   - The benchmark had a label mismatch (DE-anchor group scored 0% for every pack)
     and inflated baseline timings (sequential instead of interleaved runs).

   test_centroid's `centroid >= linear` became a 1-case tolerance with the reason
   recorded in the test: removing _cross_lingual_mixup changed the LINEAR probe's
   training set (superset), shifting its boundary on 1 of 60 frozen cases.

TESTS: 307 passed, 0 failed (was 242 passed at v0.8.8).

FOOTPRINT (Windows/CPython 3.11, MiniLM):
  glossary load, fresh process                8.5 ms   (budget 20 ms)
  glossary load, in the 3.4 GB bench process  27.4 ms  <- reported, not hidden
  1st lookup (index build, deferred)          44 ms
  RAM deep-size                               5.68 MB  (budget 10 MB)
  file size                                   0.62 MB  (budget 2 MB)

ACCURACY (evals/v090_bench.py):
  EN anchors <- DE queries (cross-lingual)  77.3% -> 86.4%   (+2 cases)
  EN anchors <- EN queries (monolingual)   100.0% -> 100.0%  (no regression)
  DE anchors <- EN queries (cross-lingual)  76.9% -> 84.6%   (+1 case)
